WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.230 --> 00:00:10.360
The NCBI Gene database is a resource that
centralizes gene related information into

00:00:10.360 --> 00:00:12.710
individual records.

00:00:12.710 --> 00:00:17.160
Many different types of gene-specific data
are connected to the record including sequence

00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:22.920
accessions, nomenclature, genomic location
and organization, publications, gene products

00:00:22.920 --> 00:00:28.660
and their attributes, expression, interactions,
pathways, homology, variation and its phenotypic

00:00:28.660 --> 00:00:34.520
consequences and useful links to databases
both internal and external to NCBI.

00:00:34.520 --> 00:00:41.020
The content of Gene is derived from both automated
dataflows and curation by NCBI staff.

00:00:41.020 --> 00:00:45.700
The starting point is typically the extraction
of gene-specific information from a publicly

00:00:45.700 --> 00:00:48.960
available, annotated genome sequence.

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:55.480
The gene is assigned a category, for example
protein coding RNA, non-coding RNA, pseudogene,

00:00:55.480 --> 00:01:02.159
ribosomal RNA or unknown based on the information
provided by each collaborating databases.

00:01:02.159 --> 00:01:08.210
• The NCBI Reference Sequence Database or
RefSeq database, is a comprehensive, integrated,

00:01:08.210 --> 00:01:14.280
non-redundant, well-annotated set of reference
sequences including genomic, transcript, and

00:01:14.280 --> 00:01:15.280
protein.

00:01:15.280 --> 00:01:19.610
Similar to a review article, a RefSeq is a
synthesis of information integrated across

00:01:19.610 --> 00:01:22.680
multiple sources at a given time.

00:01:22.680 --> 00:01:28.470
RefSeqs provide a foundation for uniting sequence
data with genetic and functional information.

00:01:28.470 --> 00:01:32.890
They are generated to provide reference standards
for multiple purposes ranging from genome

00:01:32.890 --> 00:01:38.040
annotation to reporting locations of sequence
variation in medical records.

00:01:38.040 --> 00:01:43.110
• The Gene Reference into Function database
or GenRIF database, is a short statement about

00:01:43.110 --> 00:01:44.920
the function of a gene.

00:01:44.920 --> 00:01:50.770
GeneRIFs provide a simple mechanism for allowing
scientists to add to the functional annotation

00:01:50.770 --> 00:01:54.090
of genes described in the Gene database.

00:01:54.090 --> 00:01:58.950
Each GeneRIF has a pointer to the PubMed ID
of a scientific publication that provides

00:01:58.950 --> 00:02:02.500
evidence for the statement made by the GeneRIF.

00:02:02.500 --> 00:02:07.850
• The Online Mendelian Inheritance in Man
or OMIM, is a continuously updated catalog

00:02:07.850 --> 00:02:13.379
of human genes and genetic disorders and traits,
with a particular focus on the gene-phenotype

00:02:13.379 --> 00:02:14.510
relationship.

00:02:14.510 --> 00:02:19.590
The following sections will help you understand
more about the NCBI gene database and how

00:02:19.590 --> 00:02:23.959
to navigate the database to extract the information
of your interest.

00:02:23.959 --> 00:02:29.209
The NCBI Gene database is an important resource
because it collects together in one place

00:02:29.209 --> 00:02:35.349
information on each gene and provides links
to other NCBI and related databases.

00:02:35.349 --> 00:02:40.180
This introduction includes a focus on links
to the PubMed database.

00:02:40.180 --> 00:02:44.780
Access Gene by using the drop-down menu from
any of the NCBI interfaces.

00:02:44.780 --> 00:02:50.560
Let’s look at CFTR, the gene associated
with cystic fibrosis.

00:02:50.560 --> 00:02:57.560
At this moment, there are nearly 3060 individual
Gene records referencing CFTR, of which 353

00:02:57.560 --> 00:03:00.020
come from humans.

00:03:00.020 --> 00:03:04.040
Although these records are sorted by relevance
to the search term, to be certain you have

00:03:04.040 --> 00:03:09.299
the correct genetic record, search the database
with the official symbol for your gene.

00:03:09.299 --> 00:03:13.709
Of these 96 records, this first one is the
human gene.

00:03:13.709 --> 00:03:18.189
Because this record is so large, note the
table of contents on the right hand side and

00:03:18.189 --> 00:03:24.599
the additional links to related National Library
of Medicine databases and other genetic resources.

00:03:24.599 --> 00:03:28.830
This first Summary section opens with the
official symbol and the full name of the gene

00:03:28.830 --> 00:03:33.650
as defined in the public database of the Human
Genome Organization’s, Gene Nomenclature

00:03:33.650 --> 00:03:34.650
Committee.

00:03:34.650 --> 00:03:39.409
However, as you can see from the “Also known
as” list, not everyone has used that official

00:03:39.409 --> 00:03:42.019
symbol in their publications.

00:03:42.019 --> 00:03:46.879
This gene encodes a protein and the Summary
includes information on how that protein functions

00:03:46.879 --> 00:03:50.099
and potential issues with gene mutations.

00:03:50.099 --> 00:03:52.409
The RefSeq status is “reviewed”.

00:03:52.409 --> 00:03:58.879
Thus, in this Gene record, the CFTR gene sequence
is an annotated reference sequence, curated

00:03:58.879 --> 00:04:04.540
by NCBI staff, with unique identifiers and
serving as a baseline for medical, functional,

00:04:04.540 --> 00:04:06.340
and comparative studies.

00:04:06.340 --> 00:04:10.439
Genomic context describes where the gene lives.

00:04:10.439 --> 00:04:17.269
In this case, the gene is on Chromosome 7,
the q (or long arm), in region 31.2.

00:04:17.269 --> 00:04:22.390
For information on how chromosomes are mapped,
see the article by Clare O’Connor.

00:04:22.390 --> 00:04:26.600
The Genome Reference Consortium submitted
the most recent assembly of the human reference

00:04:26.600 --> 00:04:30.890
genome to GenBank in December of 2014.

00:04:30.890 --> 00:04:35.760
Because new data and corrections have changed
CFTR’s location on the chromosome, Gene

00:04:35.760 --> 00:04:41.050
includes information on both the current and
past assembly to assist researchers.

00:04:41.050 --> 00:04:47.510
Note that the CFTR gene starts after the 117.4
millionth base pair on chromosome 7 in this

00:04:47.510 --> 00:04:49.540
current assembly.

00:04:49.540 --> 00:04:53.060
This diagram shows the direction in which
the gene is read.

00:04:53.060 --> 00:04:57.640
Remember, the double helix can be read in
either direction and convention states the

00:04:57.640 --> 00:05:01.760
chromosome be presented with the shorter arm
on the top or to the left.

00:05:01.760 --> 00:05:08.370
CFTR, then, is read toward the right, but
this nearby CTTNBP2 gene is read toward the

00:05:08.370 --> 00:05:11.720
left or the shorter arm of the chromosome.

00:05:11.720 --> 00:05:15.880
This next section provides a type of gene
browser that offers a tremendous amount of

00:05:15.880 --> 00:05:17.620
information.

00:05:17.620 --> 00:05:20.600
Click on the green line representing the gene.

00:05:20.600 --> 00:05:25.370
Now you can see information on the gene in
green, information on the transcribed messenger

00:05:25.370 --> 00:05:30.340
RNA in blue, and information on the translated
protein in red.

00:05:30.340 --> 00:05:34.870
Multiple blue and red lines represent splice
variants, variants which can generate different

00:05:34.870 --> 00:05:37.210
messenger RNAs.

00:05:37.210 --> 00:05:42.280
Experimentally verified transcripts begin
with N, while variants beginning with X represent

00:05:42.280 --> 00:05:46.520
a predicted mRNA transcript or transcribed
protein.

00:05:46.520 --> 00:05:50.990
Now let’s jump to a specific location on
the gene and zoom in.

00:05:50.990 --> 00:05:55.930
Note that the larger bars represent exons
and the smaller bars are introns.

00:05:55.930 --> 00:06:00.800
An important concept for researchers is clinical
variation, when variations in the gene have

00:06:00.800 --> 00:06:03.140
a clinical significance.

00:06:03.140 --> 00:06:07.740
The darker the color, the more pathogenic
or deadly the variation.

00:06:07.740 --> 00:06:16.400
Notice that this particular variation, rs1800111,
has a PubMed citation associated with it.

00:06:16.400 --> 00:06:21.960
Additional information in the browser includes
the gene sequence; SNPs, the Single Nucleotide

00:06:21.960 --> 00:06:26.560
Polymorphisms, or how individuals differ from
one another; and information on the exons

00:06:26.560 --> 00:06:30.770
and introns;all with links to related databases.

00:06:30.770 --> 00:06:35.030
This next section, Bibliography, needs no
introduction.

00:06:35.030 --> 00:06:40.340
These are selected PubMed citations focusing
on the human CFTR gene.

00:06:40.340 --> 00:06:46.210
For citations on the similar genes in other
species, homologs, click on this final link.

00:06:46.210 --> 00:06:51.840
If you need citations focusing on a specific
function of a gene, this section on GeneRIFs,

00:06:51.840 --> 00:06:55.330
Gene References Into Function, can be very
helpful.

00:06:55.330 --> 00:07:00.130
A single sentence is taken from the title
or abstract that concisely describes the function

00:07:00.130 --> 00:07:03.210
or functions of the gene in that article.

00:07:03.210 --> 00:07:08.060
These links will take you into PubMed, just
as PubMed citations can bring you back into

00:07:08.060 --> 00:07:09.130
Gene.

00:07:09.130 --> 00:07:12.960
There is a great deal of additional information
in the Gene record which we will cover in

00:07:12.960 --> 00:07:19.270
future videos, including phenotypes, variation
and pathways, interactions which link to PubMed

00:07:19.270 --> 00:07:24.230
citations discussing those interactions, protein
information, reference and related sequences,

00:07:24.230 --> 00:07:25.640
and resource links.

00:07:25.640 --> 00:07:31.630
I do want to highlight the Gene ontology section
which summarizes, and provides PubMed citations

00:07:31.630 --> 00:07:36.320
for the Functions, Processes and Components
known for this gene.

00:07:36.320 --> 00:07:41.210
Ontology is the study of what exists and gene
ontology is the controlled vocabulary of terms

00:07:41.210 --> 00:07:46.720
describing gene product characteristics and
is compiled in the Gene Ontology Annotation

00:07:46.720 --> 00:07:47.960
Database.

00:07:47.960 --> 00:07:52.600
As you can see, the Gene Database provides
a one-stop node to retrieve all information

00:07:52.600 --> 00:07:55.170
related to the gene of interest.

00:07:55.170 --> 00:08:00.140
If you scroll down to the mRNA sequence section,
you can also access the gene sequence from

00:08:00.140 --> 00:08:05.160
the Genbank database which primarily store
the DNA and mRNA sequence information.

